## Easing the pain

- **anti-inflammatory painkillers**, such as **ibuprofen** tablets or gel, can help with pain and swelling
- **paracetamol** can be used if you cannot take ibuprofen
- a **heat pad** or a hot water bottle wrapped in a towel on your chest can ease pain
- some people find an **ice pack** wrapped in a towel helps more

A **pharmacist** can help you choose the right painkiller and check it is safe with your other medicines.

## Rest and activity

- **avoid activities that make the pain worse**, such as heavy lifting, pushing, or sports that strain the chest, until it settles
- keep gently active, as staying completely still can make stiffness worse
- **gentle stretches** for the chest and shoulders may help once the worst pain has eased

## If it does not get better

If the pain is still bad after about **8 weeks**, see a GP. They may:

- prescribe **stronger anti-inflammatory medicines**
- refer you to **physiotherapy**, for stretches, posture advice and hands-on treatment
- offer an **injection** of a steroid or local anaesthetic into the painful area
- check again for other causes of chest pain

For long-lasting pain, your GP may suggest treatments used for **chronic pain**, such as certain medicines or a pain management programme.

Read our tips on [living well with costochondritis](/conditions/musculoskeletal/costochondritis/living-well/).
